WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.420
Stack and heap safety is a practical memory-safety discipline.

2
00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:11.560
C++ gives engineers direct control over storage duration, object lifetime, ownership, and performance.

3
00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:17.900
That control is one reason C++ is useful in systems, services, engines, and infrastructure.

4
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.640
It is also why memory decisions must be explicit enough for maintainers to review.

5
00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:29.800
Automatic storage, often described as stack storage, is commonly used for local objects whose lifetime follows scope.

6
00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:40.140
Dynamic storage, often described as heap storage, is used when an object needs a size, lifetime, or ownership model that is not tied to one simple scope.

7
00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:44.400
Neither choice is automatically safe or unsafe.

8
00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:47.700
A stack buffer can still be accessed out of bounds.

9
00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:52.040
A heap object can be managed safely through clear ownership and RAII.

10
00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:59.100
The real questions are whether the object exists, whether access stays within bounds, and who is responsible for cleanup.

11
00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:02.900
Memory safety depends on connecting these decisions.

12
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.880
Lifetime tells us when an object is valid.

13
00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:08.220
Bounds tell us which memory belongs to it.

14
00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:11.120
Ownership tells us who releases or transfers it.

15
00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:17.380
Interfaces tell reviewers whether access is owning, borrowed, short-lived, or shared.

16
00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:26.280
When those answers are hidden, defects such as stale pointers, leaks, double cleanup, and invalid buffer access become harder to catch.

17
00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:31.520
Safer C++ is not about banning the heap or avoiding every low-level construct.

18
00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:42.980
It is about making memory assumptions visible, bounded, and maintainable so performance and control do not come at the expense of reliability or security.
